<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Animals in Our Waters – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Animals in Our Waters</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Discuss plans for the future on a variety of topics. Interactive: Engage in debates on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss plans related to ocean conservation, educational visits, or recreational activities in Panama's coastal areas (e.g., "Next weekend, I am going to visit Punta Culebra to learn about sea turtle conservation." "My family is planning a trip to El Escudo de Veraguas to enjoy the beach and learn about its marine biodiversity." "In the future, I want to participate in a beach cleanup at Playa Venao to help reduce plastic waste.")
                Can engage in a debate about protecting marine life by presenting arguments and supporting evidence related to local conservation efforts (e.g., "I believe fishing limits near Coiba National Park are necessary to protect endangered fish species like the grouper and ensure sustainable fishing for future generations." "I think stricter regulations around Isla Iguanas are important because its coral reefs provide shelter for many species and attract eco-tourism during whale season, which benefits the community.")
                Can share future goals related to ocean conservation (e.g., "I am planning to join a group that cleans beaches to protect wildlife.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h4></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of Panama's coastal areas (e.g., Coiba National Park, Isla Iguana, Punta Culebra) and different marine animals (e.g., sea turtles, dolphins, coral reefs, mangroves). Students work in pairs to quickly name the animals they see and one thing they know about that place or animal. Then, a few pairs share with the class. (e.g., "That's a sea turtle. They live near Coiba.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences for discussing future plans and making suggestions related to the images. "Next weekend, I am planning to visit a beach cleanup." "We should protect coral reefs because they are home to many species." The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "ecosystem," "biodiversity," and "endangered," as well as minimal pairs like /iː/ in "reef" and /ɪ/ in "riff" by exaggerating the vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding of key terms and structures.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I am planning to clean the beach,' is that happening now or in the future?" (Expected answer: In the future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If 'endangered' means an animal's population is getting smaller, is it good or bad?" (Expected answer: Bad.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (approximately 1.5-2 minutes) between two Panamanian teenagers discussing their upcoming plans related to ocean conservation or visits to coastal areas. The dialogue includes present continuous for future plans, present simple for facts about marine life, and modals for suggestions, integrating vocabulary such as "conservation," "biodiversity," "coral reefs," "protected zones," and "beach cleanup."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Provide suggestions during discussions on marine protection and participate in debates on environmental conservation strategies (e.g., "We should establish more marine protected areas to help preserve ocean biodiversity.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (first listening):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the two teenagers mainly talking about?" (A. School, B. Their summer plans, C. Ocean conservation)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are they planning to do something related to the ocean or not?" (Yes, they are.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Questions (second listening):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific activity is one of the teenagers planning for the weekend?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What marine animals or habitats do they mention needing protection?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What suggestions do they make for protecting the ocean?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple for describing ocean life ("There are many species of fish..."), Present continuous for ongoing actions/future plans ("The sharks are swimming around the coral reef," "I am planning a trip..."), Modals for suggestions and obligations ("We should protect our oceans," "We must conserve...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> ocean, marine life, coral reefs, ecosystem, pollution, habitat, biodiversity, fish, conservation, coastal areas, preservation, protected zones; protect, conserve, preserve, clean; endangered, diverse, fragile, sustainable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "ecosystem," "biodiversity," "environmental") and minimal pairs (/iː/ in "reef" and /ɪ/ in "riff").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Proposing conservation actions, describing ecological importance.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Prompts:</b> Students receive cards with sentence prompts related to ocean conservation. In pairs, they complete the sentences orally using the target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompts: "Next month, I am planning to ____________ (verb + object) to help the ____________ (noun)." "Coral reefs ___________ (present simple verb) a ____________ (adjective) habitat for many ___________ (noun)." "We ____________ (modal) stop ____________ (verb-ing) the ____________ (noun)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors for accurate use of verb tenses and modals, correcting pronunciation of key words.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Chain Reaction Interview:</b> Students stand in a circle. The teacher starts by asking a question, e.g., "What are you planning to do for ocean conservation next year?" The first student answers, then asks the same question to the next student, and so on. They must use the present continuous for future plans.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follow-up round: "Why should we protect marine life?" Students use modals (should, must) and vocabulary like "biodiversity," "ecosystem," "fragile."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides feedback on fluency and proper use of target structures, specifically noting stress in words like "conservation" and "environment."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Make a Suggestion" Game:</b> Students work in pairs. One student describes a problem related to ocean pollution or endangered species using descriptive vocabulary (e.g., "Plastic pollution is making many marine animals endangered near our coastal areas."). The other student must respond with a suggestion using a modal verb and a conservation action (e.g., "We should organize a beach cleanup at Playa Venao." or "We must educate people about sustainable fishing."). They switch roles after a few turns.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mini-Debate: "Protecting Panama's Marine Biodiversity."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups (3-4 students). Each group is assigned a role: either to argue FOR or AGAINST a specific statement related to ocean conservation in Panama.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Debate Statements (examples):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"All fishing should be strictly prohibited in marine protected zones like Coiba National Park to fully restore fish populations." (FOR/AGAINST)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The government should invest more in eco-tourism development in coastal areas than in traditional industries like commercial fishing." (FOR/AGAINST)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group spends 5 minutes discussing and organizing their arguments, using the target vocabulary (e.g., sustainable, endangered, biodiversity, ecosystem, conservation, pollution) and grammatical structures (e.g., "I believe stricter regulations are necessary because...", "We should focus on...", "Fishing limits are important to protect species like the grouper.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then appoints a spokesperson (or shares speaking turns) to present their main arguments (1-2 minutes per side).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After initial presentations, groups can offer one rebuttal or question to the opposing side.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher facilitates and encourages interaction, focusing on fluent expression and logical argumentation, and providing gentle corrections for pronunciation and grammar as needed. Students are encouraged to use phrases for proposing actions and describing ecological importance.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the mini-debate to assess students' speaking performance, specifically their ability to discuss plans, engage in debate, present arguments, and make suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially stress in multisyllabic words and clear differentiation of minimal pairs like "reef" and "riff").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary related to ocean life and conservation (e.g., "biodiversity," "endangered," "coral reefs," "mangroves").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of grammar (present simple, present continuous for plans, modals for suggestions/obligations).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clearly propose conservation actions and describe ecological importance (Pragmatic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and respectful participation in the debate, providing suggestions and supporting arguments (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket at the end of the lesson.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel discussing plans for ocean conservation or participating in a debate about marine protection?" (Scale 1-5, 1=not confident, 5=very confident)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one new phrase or word you used today to talk about 'The Animals in Our Waters' or 'Ocean Life and Conservation'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about debating or discussing conservation plans today, and how can I improve my speaking for next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using words like 'ecosystem' or 'endangered' help me express my ideas better in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively use the present continuous for future plans and modals for suggestions/obligations during the activities and debate?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the mini-debate format effective for encouraging interactive speaking at the A2.4 level? What adjustments are needed for students to formulate more complex arguments?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students integrate the target vocabulary and practice pronunciation of multisyllabic words and minimal pairs?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific scaffolding (e.g., more sentence starters, vocabulary review, listening examples) is needed to support quieter students in contributing to speaking tasks in the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson provided opportunities for students to express opinions and make suggestions about ocean conservation. In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will synthesize these ideas into short written paragraphs, composing their own opinion pieces or conservation proposals based on the topics discussed today, using the same vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>